"Highlights the signed gap between two aligned quantitative series across time.\n\nUse when: Two directly comparable series share timestamps and measurement units. The duration and magnitude of one exceeding the other matter.\n\nAvoid when: Series use different units or a second axis would be required.\n\nVisual grammar: The two boundaries encode the original series values. Fill between boundaries encodes their difference; hue indicates which series is larger.\n\nBuild: Align timestamps and document interpolation or missing-value handling. Draw both measured series on one quantitative scale. Fill only the interval between them, splitting regions at true intersections.\n\nCheck: Check both series use the same units and reporting intervals. Verify sign changes occur at correct crossings. Confirm the fill does not extend to zero unless zero is an actual series boundary.\n\nPitfalls: The boundaries must cross where the values cross; changing routing corrupts the data. Use a separate signed-difference line when the filled regions become fragmented or hard to decode.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic daily planned versus actual demand, shading above-plan and below-plan gaps with a clear sign legend.\n\nAlternatives: Diverging bar chart, Line chart, Area chart."
